186498662 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498696 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498662 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498708 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498662 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498662 1077 "selection" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498662 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498686 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498662 1079 #uuid "030b1280-3f72-5e40-9687-4451485960c3" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498663 1080 "$A$ and $C$ only" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498663 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498663 1083 #uuid "0944b8cd-a8ad-5881-872d-cc7841b124b3" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498664 1080 "$B$ only" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498664 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498664 1083 #uuid "0a037906-7560-5a7f-af3a-df39ea31c7f5" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498665 1080 "$B$ only" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498665 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498665 1083 #uuid "0b01faa9-1661-5d2c-8b15-89c1d529166b" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498716 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498666 1071 "![](images/4c/4c714262b9da22ea9ad859817db86a3aaf0b29ef811246aedc969bc9999c0348.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=x+3.$" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498666 1072 #uuid "0f863f32-c463-5f07-9482-1c6f6e74c019" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498666 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-3,0)$, $B(-1,4)$ and $C(3,6).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-3,0)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+3 \\\\ (0) & =1(-3)+3 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,4)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+3 \\\\ (4) & =1(-1)+3 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(3,6)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+3 \\\\ (6) & =1(3)+3 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line." 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498661 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498667 1071 "![](images/3c/3cee718f18c9ef081b3e90a70d4db00ef0339d7d5f0b87423f7c5687709471a6.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-5x-2?$" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498667 1072 #uuid "12a60b9c-85ce-50af-b45d-06caed36a198" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498667 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,9),B(-1,4)$ and $C(1,-7).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,9)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x-2 \\\\ (9) & =-5(-2)-2 \\\\ 9 & =8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does *not* lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,4)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x-2 \\\\ (4) & =-5(-1)-2 \\\\ 4 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,-7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x-2 \\\\ (-7) & =-5(1)-2 \\\\ -7 & =-7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line." 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498668 1080 "$A$ and $B$ only" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498668 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498668 1083 #uuid "1b24e4af-347a-5ac2-9514-dd6e178db687" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498715 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498669 1071 "![](images/70/70b04ef6ee89c2bf06db99444f1f89afbe2ea15871f8f79da29668f7be3f9f97.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x.$" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498669 1072 #uuid "21a9c497-cf26-5338-8f2e-52de8c6489fc" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498669 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,-2)$, $B(1,4)$ and $C(2,4).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\ (-2) & =2(-1)+0 \\\\ -2 & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,4)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\ (4) & =2(1)+0 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,4)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\ (4) & =2(2)+0 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line." 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498670 1080 "$C$ only" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498670 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498670 1083 #uuid "2517ee62-3508-5043-99a6-8cbff4a054bf" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498671 1080 "$A$ and $B$ only" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498671 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498671 1083 #uuid "2773d5bd-6a1e-5e0f-bdc4-1f27d274f0fc" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498662 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498672 1071 "![](images/eb/eb6b3f51e0e61f72015505469f9fd6e6aa2d5e5b550825e4c761fc78f535ef59.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=3x+2.$" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498672 1072 #uuid "277a1bc9-d733-576e-9af4-fb70571bb178" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498672 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-3,-7)$, $B(-1,1)$ and $C(1,5).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-3,-7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+2 \\\\ (-7) & =3(-3)+2 \\\\ -7 & =-7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+2 \\\\ (1) & =3(-1)+2 \\\\ 1 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,5)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+2 \\\\ (5) & =3(1)+2 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line." 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498673 1080 "$A$ and $B$ only" 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498673 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535342 17592186045443 true] [17592186498673 1083 #uuid "2d413d8c-3f61-5d64-83db-8f5043